WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
React Native makes a highly appealing promise to web developers.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.800
The React Logic you already know, your hooks, your state management, your context,

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.100
snaps seamlessly straight onto a mobile device.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.800
But if you take a standard React Web component and paste it directly into a React Native file,

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.200
your app will immediately crash.

6
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.000
The underlying React Logic is identical,

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
but the UI primitives you use to build the interface are strictly incompatible.

8
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.800
This split-screen diagram highlights why.

9
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.300
On the left, the web DOM is incredibly forgiving.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.300
Browsers will implicitly guess how to render messy or incomplete HTML tags

11
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.400
and automatically format them into something readable.

12
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.100
Try that in React Native and you get a harsh render error.

13
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.400
Native iOS and Android engines do not guess.

14
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.600
They require strict explicit instructions to draw every single pixel on the screen.

15
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.900
Components like view and text have to be locked perfectly into predefined slots.

16
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.600
Moving from web to mobile means retraining your mental model.

17
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.200
You have to stop relying on the implicit flow of a web document

18
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.500
and start explicitly defining a native UI architecture.

19
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.400
Your first major swap is throwing out semantic HTML containers.

20
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.400
Headers, mains, and divs condense into a single React Native component,

21
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.600
the view.

22
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.500
Without web document flow, these view blocks won't automatically stack,

23
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.200
operating entirely on a strict flexbox layout.

24
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.200
You have to explicitly define the flexbox directions and boundaries

25
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.400
for every single container because there are absolutely no default block behaviors to rely on.

26
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.200
That strictness extends to typography.

27
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.500
On the web, you can leave a string of text floating loose inside a div.

28
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.200
On mobile, you can't.

29
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.600
This animation shows exactly what happens if you try.

30
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.800
The floating text string triggers a hard crash.

31
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:05.300
Every single piece of text must be explicitly wrapped in a text component for the app to function.

32
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.800
iOS and Android rendering engines require these precise text nodes

33
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.500
so they can accurately calculate typography bounds

34
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.300
and apply system-level accessibility scaling.

35
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.300
In this environment, you claim and define the rules for every element on the screen.

36
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.700
The platform gives you nothing by default.

37
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.200
User interactions follow this exact same requirement.

38
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.500
On the web, you can slap an on-click attribute onto almost any element and call it a day.

39
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.400
To make anything interactive in React Native, you must wrap it in a pressable component.

40
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.400
This explicit wrapper provides the direct hook for native touch feedback,

41
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.900
offering a generous touch target that registers off-center tops and tactile long press dates.

42
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.300
But you do lose standard web routing.

43
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.100
Traditional href links and URL-based routing simply do not exist natively.

44
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.700
Navigation becomes an imperative action.

45
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.500
You have to manually trigger routing functions,

46
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.000
telling the app exactly when and where to navigate.

47
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.900
Native interactions prioritize tactile, physical state changes

48
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.000
over the web's simple document linking model.

49
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.100
Now we arrive at the most computationally expensive UI task in mobile development,

50
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.700
scrolling through massive lists of data.

51
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.500
In the standard web approach, mapping an array into standard HTML list tags

52
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.000
loads the entire list into the DOM simultaneously.

53
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.300
On mobile hardware, this causes severe memory bloat and frame drops.

54
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.400
The device cannot hold that much unrendered UI.

55
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.700
To render arrays safely, you use a native component called flat list.

56
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:42.300
It uses virtualization, dynamically rendering only items visible on the physical screen.

57
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.100
Items moving off screen are deleted, which immediately drops the memory load back down to safe levels.

58
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.900
The trade-off for this performance is extra code.

59
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:57.000
Instead of a simple map function, you have to write data arrays, render functions and key extractors.

60
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.100
On mobile, you gladly trade that code simplicity

61
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:05.100
for the absolute memory efficiency required to keep the app running smoothly.

62
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.000
When it comes to data collection,

63
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.600
React Native completely drops the standard HTML form tag.

64
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.700
Without it, you lose browser native conveniences like automatic input grouping,

65
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.000
required fields and default submit behaviors.

66
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Here, you manually connect individual text input components directly to a central React state manager to track typing.

67
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.300
You also handle physical constraints.

68
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.900
When the mobile keyboard slides up, you must program the UI to shift upward,

69
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.600
preventing inputs from hiding behind keys.

70
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.300
You've left browser handled forms behind.

71
00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.700
Every mobile form is a custom built state machine controlled entirely by your code.

72
00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.600
Moving from web to React Native is a strict negotiation.

73
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.700
You are abandoning implicit browser guesses to provide explicit pixel-perfect instructions.

74
00:04:55.700 --> 00:05:00.200
This decision tree maps the two paths for a team evaluating this transition.

75
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.500
React Native makes sense for teams demanding high fidelity performance,

76
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.200
granular memory control and native app store distribution,

77
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.800
and who have the budget to rewrite their UI.

78
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.500
However, if your team has zero budget for a UI refactor,

79
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.300
or if your goal is getting your exact website onto a user's home screen,

80
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.500
a native rewrite is the wrong choice.

81
00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.900
In that case, wrap your app in a progressive web app shell.

82
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.600
You sacrifice performance, but save massive development time.

83
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.400
Your React logic is universal, but the rendering constraints are not.

84
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.200
Respecting those strict explicit native constraints

85
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.200
is the only way to build an app that actually feels like it belongs on a phone.

